How it works:
- Reads `{old_id}.note`; if missing (or the file's internal id field doesn't match `old_id`) it returns a `NotFound` error before touching anything, so a failed rename has zero side effects.
- Guards against clobbering: if `new_id` differs and `{new_id}.note` already exists, it returns `AlreadyExists` without writing. If `new_id == old_id` it's a pure title change in place.
- Re-serializes using the exact same `id / SEP / title / SEP / pinned / SEP / body` format as `save`, carrying the pinned flag and body over verbatim — so tags (derived from the body on load) are unchanged by construction, not recomputed.
- Writes the new file, then removes the old one, so `load_all` sees exactly one note under the new id with no trace of the old id on disk.

Verified by real execution: `cargo test` → 7/7 pass (including the acceptance cases: exactly one note back with new id/title, body/tags/pinned preserved, old file gone; plus missing-id, collision, same-id, and save-round-trip cases). `cargo fmt --check` clean, `cargo build` clean, and `git diff --stat` in the run root shows only the two intended files changed.


